# GitHub Linguist classification.
#
# Fors itself is NOT yet a language Linguist knows: its threshold is ~2000
# files per extension indexed across many unique repositories in the last
# year, and this repository is private, so GitHub's index sees no `.fors`
# files at all. `*.fors linguist-language=Fors` cannot work until Fors is
# registered upstream, which is a post-v0.1, post-public question. What this
# file does today is stop the language bar from describing the project as
# something it is not.

# Benchmark baselines are fixtures, not this project's source. Ten
# toolchains' implementations of the same kernels otherwise make the
# repository look like a polyglot application; they are the referee's
# measuring sticks.
bench/kernels/** linguist-vendored

# The reference parser is a deliberate SECOND implementation whose only job
# is to disagree with the Rust one. It is source, and it stays counted.

# Generated artefacts: regenerating them must never move the language bar.
docs/spec/PACK.md            linguist-generated
crates/fors-asm/tests/golden/** linguist-generated

# The conformance corpus is Fors source. Once Fors is registered upstream
# these are the files that should dominate the bar, which is the point of
# keeping them out of `linguist-vendored`.

# Line endings: the corpus contains deliberate CRLF fixtures (the formatter
# and the LSP both have tests for them), so git must not normalise anything.
* -text
